    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok so i get that it makes hashes different. but can you explain how that actually stops the rainbow table thing specifically? like how does the salt break the precomputed part?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure thing! When you use a rainbow table to crack a password, it relies on precomputed hash values of common passwords. If multiple users use the same password, their hashes will also match the ones in the rainbow table, making it easier to crack.\n\nSalting changes this game. By adding a random, unique salt value to each password before hashing, even if users use the same original password, their hashes will be different. This means that a single rainbow table won't work for cracking those passwords. Attackers must generate a new rainbow table for each unique salt, which is a significant and impractical task, especially considering the hash function and salt size usually used in password storage. This additional step makes it significantly harder and time-consuming for attackers to crack the passwords, thereby enhancing security.",
      "source": "target",
